  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nMidday satellite imagery depicted remaining stratus dissolving\nrapidly, and surface observations outlined the warm front making\nquick progress northward into Kansas. Expecting a large\ntemperature gradient to result by 4 pm, ranging from 80 north to\nnear 100 along the Oklahoma border. Mesoanalysis outlined\nsignificant instability south of the warm front, and 12z NAM\nforecasts CAPE to reach near 3000 J/kg by peak heating,\nsupported by dewpoints well into the 60s. Models have also\nreduced the amount of convective inhibition at peak heating, \nwith 700 mb temperatures a tad cooler. As such, there will \nlikely be at least an attempt at an isolated thunderstorm Sunday\nevening, particularly toward sunset as the low level jet ramps \nup. 12z NAM/ARW suggests this may be near DDC, but with very \nlittle confidence, the forecast is dry (pops < 15%). The low \nlevel jet will keep south winds elevated through Monday morning,\nwith many locations remaining above 70 degrees.\n\nMonday will be the hottest day of this forecast period, with\nrecord highs for September 14th likely. The numbers to beat at\nthe three climate sites are DDC 100/2017, GCK 101/1955, P28\n104/1911. Pressure gradients will support strong southwest winds\nMonday midday and afternoon, and wind grids follow the strongest\nguidance. 12z MAV forecasts 30 mph sustained winds Monday across\nmuch of SW KS, with a well mixed boundary layer supporting gusts\nof 40-45 mph. Downslope compression will encourage temperatures\nto reach record levels. Heat indices will be near heat advisory\ncriteria (105) for a few hours Monday afternoon, but not\nsufficient for an advisory at this time. \n\nScattered thunderstorms are expected to develop along the lee\ntrough after 4 pm Monday, from Baca county Colorado to the\nnorthwest zones. NBM pops favoring the northwest zones were\naccepted. A hot mixed boundary layer coupled with strong ambient\nsynoptic winds will promote a primary risk of outflow winds to\nnear 60 mph, supporting SPC's 5% marginal wind probability\nnorthern zones. \n\nA rather strong cold front for September standards is forecast\nto arrive in NW KS Tuesday morning, with 12z NAM showing a\nsharp cold frontal passage southward through SW KS daylight\nTuesday. Emanating from a 1025 mb surface high over Nebraska,\nnortheast winds will be quite strong by late Tuesday, well above\nNBM guidance. Temperature forecasting for any specific location\nTuesday will be very difficult, with even the NBM depicting an\nimpressive 30 degree gradient across the DDC CWA (near 70 north,\nnear 100 south). Exact high temperatures in between could really\nbe anything based on frontal timing, with a non-diurnal curve\ndownward in the afternoon after frontal passage. Getting more\nfall-like!\n\nThunderstorms are expected to easily develop along the sharp\nfrontal boundary Tuesday afternoon. It is expected for SPC\nsevere probabilities for wind/hail to increase some for Tuesday,\nalthough models currently suggest instability may be limited\nalong the strong convergence zone. Trends continue to suggest\nmore widespread showers and thunderstorms Tuesday night, as\noverrunning/warm air advection initiate north of the front. \nNBM pops remain likely to categorical (60-80%) and these were\naccepted. Some significant rainfall totals are possible Tuesday\nnight. \n\nWednesday and Thursday, expectation is for the frontal boundary\nto retreat northward again, as a trough enters the Pacific NW,\nand the strong 598 dm ridge remains anchored over the\nMississippi valley. Elevated south winds and above normal\ntemperatures are expected to prevail in this synoptic regime. \nAny thunderstorms Wednesday/Thursday evenings would favor the\nnorthwest zones, furthest from the influence of the sprawling\nupper ridge.",
